WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.230
Well, we just started 2024, and with this
video, I'll have to update the list of countries

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.779
where my channel is officially banned.

00:00:06.779 --> 00:00:11.300
Remember that robot dog that, back in 2020,
was making sure people in Singapore followed

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.599
social distancing?

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:18.450
Well, its use was promoted by this man with
the creepy smile: Lee Hsien Loong, the Prime

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:19.610
Minister of Singapore...

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:21.109
since 2004.

00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:25.240
Lee is part of the People's Action Party,
which has continuously governed the Asian

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.290
City-State since 1959.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:32.550
After all, Singapore wouldn't be what it is
today without Lee's father, Lee Kuan Yew,

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:38.350
the first historic head of government, considered
the founder of the nation.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:49.250
Streets, schools, and public buildings are
all named after him, and when he died in 2015,

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:53.949
he was given a state funeral that would make
the Kim dynasty and Queen Elizabeth look pale

00:00:53.949 --> 00:00:54.949
in comparison.

00:00:54.949 --> 00:01:07.350
Add to that 
the fact that the Lees are two of only three

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:11.650
men to have ever served as Prime Ministers
of Singapore, and that the People’s Action

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:17.050
Party has been winning every election for
sixty years with more than 60% of the vote.

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:19.439
On paper, Singapore is a democracy.

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:23.759
However, this democracy is often described
as authoritarian, illiberal, bureaucratic,

00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:31.570
or, in some cases, a soft dictatorship.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:44.210
Nah, he must have misspoken.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:48.750
Otherwise, it wouldn't explain why whole fleets
of influencers visit Singapore and describe

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.380
it as a paradise on Earth,

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:55.659
A utopian tech island where about six million
people live in peace, wealth, and total security.

00:01:55.659 --> 00:01:59.149
And i could tell you more, they seem to be
right.

00:01:59.149 --> 00:02:03.430
Singapore enjoys one of the lowest crime rates
in the world, perceived corruption is virtually

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:09.709
non-existent, unemployment is stable at 2%,
and 90% of residents own at least one home.

00:02:09.709 --> 00:02:14.650
Moreover, this small Asian state has one of
the best healthcare systems on the planet,

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:19.019
combined with a public education system that
puts the Western one to shame, as well as

00:02:19.019 --> 00:02:22.069
an average life expectancy that exceeds 84
years.

00:02:22.069 --> 00:02:27.700
To top it all off, Singapore has four nature
reserves, cutting-edge transport, green technologies

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:32.620
and facilities, an efficient waste management
system, and impressive tourist attractions.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.720
Maybe, the data will give you an even clearer
idea of how Singapore is doing.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:42.370
With a GDP per capita of 87 thousand dollars,
Singaporeans are better off than Qataris,

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.130
Canadians, and Icelanders.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.319
Their average salary is close to 6000 dollars
a month.

00:02:47.319 --> 00:02:51.959
If we look at GDP, just under 500 billion
dollars, we'd find that this dot on the Asian

00:02:51.959 --> 00:02:57.330
maps is a richer and more productive country
than demographic giants like Egypt, Nigeria,

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:01.840
and Pakistan, and nations considered wealthy
such as Finland, New Zealand, and Luxembourg.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.390
So, where do you apply to move there?

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:08.670
On the website of the Singapore Ministry of
Manpower, or by taking part in the Global

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:09.900
Investor Programme:

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.069
you just need to invest 2.5 million dollars
in the country's development.

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:19.849
No wonder Singapore is home to 240 thousand
millionaires – making up almost 5% of the

00:03:19.849 --> 00:03:22.420
population – and 46 billionaires.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:27.510
Among them, the richest is Eduardo Saverin,
one of the co-founders of Facebook, who recently

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:30.440
obtained Singaporean citizenship despite being
Brazilian.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:32.120
"Ah, le pouvoir de l'argent."

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.540
Ah, the power of money.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.620
To apply for citizenship, you need to live
in Singapore for at least two years and demonstrate

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:42.290
excellent entrepreneurial skills and/or family
ties with another Singaporean.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:46.040
The downside is that dual citizenship is not
allowed, so you’ll have to give up your

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:47.040
other passport.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.840
Not a big deal, given that – currently - the
Singaporean passport is the strongest in the

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:52.840
world.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.690
If you have it, you can visit 193 countries
without any visa.

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:59.560
Watch out however, In Singapore, not everyone
is treated equally.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:03.370
Foreign migrant workers, especially in the
fields of manufacturing and domestic cleaning,

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:08.330
are often underpaid or not paid at all, deprived
of their passports, abused, forced to work

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:11.810
without holidays or sick leave, and housed
in crowded dormitories

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:17.150
This modern form of slavery reportedly affects
at least 9,000 people and is carried out by

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:21.919
actual agencies that recruit staff mainly
from Indonesia, the Philippines, and Myanmar

00:04:21.919 --> 00:04:24.280
to contribute to Singapore's development.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.639
Not Part of This Category and have the privilege
to not worry?

00:04:27.639 --> 00:04:28.639
Great.

00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:29.680
Suppose you manage to get a document.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:30.729
Where would you go and live?

00:04:30.729 --> 00:04:35.860
Can I suggest the urban division of Marine
Parade, a cheerful residential area that didn't

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:37.650
even exist until the 1970s.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:43.240
Yes, because in recent decades, Singapore
has extended its original surface by 25%,

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:47.889
simply by reclaiming swamps, building along
the coasts, and throwing rocks and sand into

00:04:47.889 --> 00:04:50.770
the sea to create new pieces of land from
nothing.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:54.830
For example, the iconic Marina Bay Sands,
one of the world's largest casino-resorts,

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:59.800
the island of Jurong, an important petrochemical
hub, and Singapore's jungle-airport were built

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.550
on so-called lands reclaimed by the government.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:06.530
To lead a peaceful life in Singapore, you
have to follow some basic rules that, frankly,

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:08.009
don’t sound too bad.

00:05:08.009 --> 00:05:10.840
You can't smoke in public, except in designated
areas.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.680
You know that terrible stench that plagues
some train stations?

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.900
Well, in Singapore, say goodbye to that.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:20.069
You can't spit on the streets,

00:05:20.069 --> 00:05:24.759
nor litter.

00:05:24.759 --> 00:05:29.190
If I think about it, I'd readily punish those
who keep on loitering, really.

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:32.840
You can't bother a person by singing or playing
a musical instrument.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.569
Leave your mandolin at home.

00:05:34.569 --> 00:05:36.280
You can't feed pigeons,

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:40.600
You can't leave a public bathroom dirty, nor
cross outside of pedestrian crossings.

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:45.620
It's about common sense, things that in a
certain country, starting with an I and ending

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:48.340
in a Y… are getting rare to find.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:50.370
I don’t think I made it clear enough.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.370
These aren’t just guide rules to follow,
but actual laws,

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:56.020
punishable by fines or even imprisonment.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:59.180
Singapore is known as the "city of fines"
for a reason.

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:00.600
Throw trash on the street?

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:01.860
That's a thousand-dollar fine.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.090
Do it again?

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:15.120
Two thousand dollars, and I'll send you to
pick up trash in a public park.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:16.120
Spit on the street?

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:18.110
The first time, a thousand dollars is fine.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:21.919
Do it multiple times, and it's from five thousand
dollars upwards.

00:06:21.919 --> 00:06:23.590
Cross the street outside of pedestrian crossings?

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:27.319
Give me 500 dollars, and you also get three
months in prison.

00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:28.319
Caught again?

00:06:28.319 --> 00:06:34.910
Two thousand dollars.

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:40.050
If you travel on any public transport, stay
in hotels, or walk in certain areas, eating

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:41.490
durian is prohibited.

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:46.120
Durian is a spiny fruit typical of Southeast
Asia that, once opened, emits a nauseating

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:51.120
stench that makes aunt Margherita's armpits
after a cycling session smell like desert

00:06:51.120 --> 00:07:00.050
roses.

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:13.729
Moreover, since 1992 in Singapore, it's impossible
to find, buy, produce, or even import chewing

00:07:13.729 --> 00:07:18.360
gum, deemed guilty of dirtying bus and metro
seats, floors, and even streets.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:21.259
In this case, penalties can soar up to one
hundred thousand

00:07:21.259 --> 00:07:23.509
dollars and include two years in prison.

00:07:23.509 --> 00:07:26.919
You know all those black dots that plague
the streets of our cities?

00:07:26.919 --> 00:07:29.370
They are flattened and blackened chewing gums.

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:31.699
Imagine, famous words by John Lennon.

00:07:31.699 --> 00:07:34.860
Imagine a clean city where the guy who spits
gum on the street is punished.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:37.440
This Singaporean dystopia is kinda growing
on me.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:42.539
Believe it or not, cleanliness and order are
pillars of Singapore's national identity.

00:07:42.539 --> 00:07:46.050
To understand why, just take a quick step
back in time.

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:51.520
The city of fines boasts a millennia-long
history, inextricably linked to that of Malaysia.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:57.020
However, the story of modern-day Singapore
begins in 1819, when the British East India

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:01.509
Company, seeking a port near the Strait of
Malacca, a trade route then controlled by

00:08:01.509 --> 00:08:05.419
the Dutch, identified an island that suited
their needs.

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:10.139
Having signed a treaty with the Sultanate
of Johor, a political entity located in the

00:08:10.139 --> 00:08:14.889
south of the Malay Peninsula, the British
obtained the island as a concession and retained

00:08:14.889 --> 00:08:18.520
its original name: Singa Pura, the Lion City.

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:21.990
And there's no doubt the British knew how
to make lemonade out of lemons.

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:27.389
Immediately, the port of Singapore was made
tax-free, meaning devoid of duties and tolls.

00:08:27.389 --> 00:08:32.360
As a result, Chinese, Malay, and Indian merchants,
as well as European businessmen, found it

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:35.640
convenient to pass through, trade, and even
live there.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:41.490
Once incorporated as a colony of the Crown
in 1867, parallel to the opening of the Suez

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:46.420
Canal, Singapore served as a crucial hub for
exporting rubber and tin then taken from Malaysia

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:47.420
to Europe.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:51.100
On the other hand, however, the Lion City
was invaded by criminal associations, slave

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:56.080
traders, while the local population, which
reached 200,000 at the turn of the 1900s,

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:57.630
lived in terrible conditions.

00:08:57.630 --> 00:08:59.600
And it didn’t get any better.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:03.070
In 1942, the Japanese Empire occupied Singapore.

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:05.529
Three years of terror and repression followed.

00:09:05.529 --> 00:09:10.430
The local society had already disintegrated,
torn apart by violence and opium, food and

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:15.600
drinking water were scarce, and most families
lived in so-called kampongs, slums and stilt

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:19.070
houses divided by rigid ethnic-religious boundaries.

00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:23.920
In this seemingly unsolvable chaos of issues,
the Singaporeans entrusted the country to

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:24.920
a charismatic leader.

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:30.370
In 1954, Lee Kuan Yew, a lawyer of Chinese
origins, educated in London, founded the People’s

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:36.529
Action Party, which in 1959 won the country's
first democratic elections by a landslide.

00:09:36.529 --> 00:09:40.920
After all, three-quarters of the Singaporean
population had – and still have – Chinese

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:41.920
origins.

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:46.290
Nonetheless, as Prime Minister, Lee promoted
equality for all citizens from the start,

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:51.410
so much so that even today Singapore recognises
four official languages: Mandarin Chinese,

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:56.769
English, Malay, and Tamil Indian, each representative
of the country's major communities.

00:09:56.769 --> 00:10:01.510
In 1960, Lee Kuan Yew's party established
the Housing and Development Board, an almost

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:03.810
independent entity aimed at constructing buildings.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:08.420
This was to ensure order and acceptable living
conditions for the now million Singaporean

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:09.510
citizens.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:15.510
In just five years, a staggering 400,000 people
moved into government-funded apartments.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:20.149
The same “reclaimed lands” we just talked
about were used over time to build new neighborhoods

00:10:20.149 --> 00:10:22.200
and housing blocks.

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:27.800
Even today, the real estate market in Singapore
is protected.

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:33.410
80% of the population lives in state buildings,
and buying them is made possible because all

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:38.210
citizens under 55 must contribute 20% of their
salary to funds.

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:42.339
From these funds, they can withdraw money
solely for buying a house, or rather, acquiring

00:10:42.339 --> 00:10:43.760
the right to live there.

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:47.709
However, urban planning had to be accompanied
by the care of public affairs.

00:10:47.709 --> 00:10:52.760
In 1968, Lee Kuan Yew's People's Action Party
launched the campaign "Keep Singapore Clean".

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:58.070
This initiative was aimed at encouraging Singaporeans
to absolutely use trash bins, discouraging

00:10:58.070 --> 00:10:59.590
littering through fines.

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:04.860
At the time, Lee stated: "No other hallmark
of our success will be more evident than becoming

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.820
the greenest and cleanest city in all of South
Asia."

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:12.540
Since then, "Keep Singapore Clean" has been
periodically updated with posters and advertisements

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:15.470
to continually remind the population of these
measures.

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:19.950
Over time, these media campaigns have also
been used to influence the will of citizens:

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:21.529
“Courtesy is our way of life,"

00:11:21.529 --> 00:11:23.360
“Let’s save precious water!"

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:25.870
“I can speak Mandarin, Mandarin’s In.

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:26.870
Dialect’s Out.”

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:29.140
“Girl or Boy, two is enough,"

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:42.100
"The second can wait."

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:54.110
We have an example of a poster like this one
from the Singapore police.

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:56.620
"It says, 'If you molest, we will arrest.'

00:11:56.620 --> 00:11:57.899
Sounds in the norm, right?

00:11:57.899 --> 00:12:02.111
There's a catch: in Singapore, being nude
in your own home is also considered a form

00:12:02.111 --> 00:12:03.980
of harassment.

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:09.080
Let me explain.

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:13.460
If you, unfortunately, expose yourself to
the public while in your underwear – say,

00:12:13.460 --> 00:12:16.610
through your bathroom window – you could
be charged with public indecency.

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:21.110
And it's possible that a police officer could
burst into your home and slap you with a hefty

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:22.110
$2,000 fine.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:23.370
But wait a minute.

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:25.980
'...up to three years in jail, a fine, and/or
caning'?

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:27.670
I need to explain this too.

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:32.600
In Singapore, as well as in Malaysia and Brunei,
caning with a rattan cane, a type of tropical

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:36.190
palm, was introduced as a punishment during
the British colonial period.

00:12:36.190 --> 00:12:41.610
Today, it's prescribed for about thirty different
charges: harassment obviously, kidnapping,

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:46.600
theft, abuse, extortion, drug trafficking,
and other serious crimes that can also carry

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:47.600
the death penalty."

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:51.370
But let's get back to caning, because things
really get interesting here.

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:57.110
According to a 2019 survey, nearly half of
all Singaporean parents use small sticks to

00:12:57.110 --> 00:13:00.610
punish disrespectful, disobedient, or lying
children.

00:13:00.610 --> 00:13:05.060
As stated by the Ministry of Education, the
same is done in schools, but only in cases

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:06.670
when "absolutely necessary.”

00:13:06.670 --> 00:13:08.610
What that means, only they know.

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:11.760
in Singapore, one can easily be caned for
vandalism.

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:17.029
This happened to Jolovan Wham, an activist
who, in 2017, hung flyers on a train and,

00:13:17.029 --> 00:13:20.810
along with colleagues, traveled with a book
and a blindfold over their eyes.

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:28.370
In 2021, Wham was fined $1,000 for vandalism,
8 strokes of a rattan cane, and $4,500 for

00:13:28.370 --> 00:13:33.050
unauthorized public assembly, penalties converted
to 22 days in prison.

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:37.519
Under the Public Order Act of 2009, you can't
organise a demonstration or gathering of any

00:13:37.519 --> 00:13:41.040
kind without first getting permission from
the authorities.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:43.709
But let's take another look at the formidable
train protest.

00:13:43.709 --> 00:13:48.330
The flyers and the book they were holding
referred to Operation Spectrum, a mass arrest

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:53.860
of 22 people carried out without trial by
the Singaporean government in 1987 to thwart

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:56.009
an alleged "Marxist conspiracy."

00:13:56.009 --> 00:14:00.490
At the time, activists were accused of attempting
to overthrow the Singaporean state system

00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:01.490
through a coup.

00:14:01.490 --> 00:14:07.040
However, it is widely believed that Lee and
his peers used an excuse to silence inconvenient

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:08.040
opponents.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:09.040
It's not surprising.

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:12.009
The Singapore government has been suppressing
dissent for decades.

00:14:12.009 --> 00:14:16.670
When Lee Kuan Yew's party came to power in
'59, they promised that Singapore would soon

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:19.350
achieve federal unification with Malaysia.

00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:23.480
Singapore, lacking significant natural resources
and being small in size, was on the brink

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:24.480
of collapse.

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:29.820
Even the British, who still had formal control
over the island, were pushing for the union.

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:34.300
Some socialist members of the People’s Action
Party, however, feared that once unification

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:39.609
was complete, Malaysia would help Lee Kuan
Yew eliminate any opposition to his power.

00:14:39.609 --> 00:14:42.310
And you certainly can't maintain order if
you have opponents.

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:48.230
So, in February 1963, Lee launched Operation
Coldstore, another mass arrest with dubious

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:52.190
intentions, aimed at "paralyzing the communist
organization."

00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:56.360
Over a hundred people suspected of plotting
an armed coup were detained.

00:14:56.360 --> 00:15:01.360
With the Socialist Front crippled, the PAP
won an absolute majority in parliament in

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:05.570
September, gained total independence from
the United Kingdom, and completed unification

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:06.570
with Malaysia.

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:07.769
Want to know the irony?

00:15:07.769 --> 00:15:11.889
Due to political and racial tensions between
the Chinese majority in Singapore and the

00:15:11.889 --> 00:15:16.240
Malaysian political class, and fears that
Singapore's port would become more important

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:22.300
than Kuala Lumpur's, in 1967, the Malaysian
parliament voted to expel Singapore from the

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:23.300
federation.

00:15:23.300 --> 00:15:27.779
Left alone by both its neighbours and the
British, the City-State of Singapore was practically

00:15:27.779 --> 00:15:30.459
forced to declare itself an autonomous Republic.

00:15:30.459 --> 00:15:42.279
Now, imagine being head of a small, country,
lacking resources.

00:15:42.279 --> 00:15:43.320
What next?

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:44.940
Well, you get creative.

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:49.870
Firstly, lacking an army in '65, Lee requested
technical support from some Israeli military

00:15:49.870 --> 00:15:50.870
officers.

00:15:50.870 --> 00:15:54.540
Tel Aviv, in fact, materially helped Singapore
build its armed forces.

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:59.180
Secondly, Lee facilitated the creation of
a series of state companies that would produce

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:04.300
oil and steel, build and repair ships, import
food and timber, and in recent decades, move

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:08.510
into financial services, sustainable technologies,
and biomedical engineering.

00:16:08.510 --> 00:16:12.839
Finally, he encouraged foreign investments
through favorable tax policies and the always

00:16:12.839 --> 00:16:16.970
convenient Port of Singapore, which is currently
the busiest in the world.

00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:21.839
Today, both China and the United States invest
in Singapore, making the City-State the most

00:16:21.839 --> 00:16:27.579
important geopolitical piece in ASEAN, the
Association of Southeast Asian Nations.

00:16:27.579 --> 00:16:31.971
And that's why Singapore is the main financial
hub of Asia, and the third globally, after

00:16:31.971 --> 00:16:34.170
New York and London.

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:38.580
Let's give Lee his due: the nation's father
understood sixty years ago that, instead of

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:41.899
isolating and turning Singapore into his personal
playground,

00:16:41.899 --> 00:16:44.990
like our old friend from Turkmenistan do you
remember him?

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:50.070
Well, it would be better to broaden horizons
and diversify the country's economy as much

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:51.079
as possible.

00:16:51.079 --> 00:16:53.860
Lee Kuan Yew doesn't seem to have been just
a prime minister.

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:58.950
The same goes for his colleagues: his son,
Hsien Loong , and Goh Chok Tong, who served

00:16:58.950 --> 00:17:00.990
from 1990 to 2004.

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:06.250
Rather, the head of the government is the
CEO of a company, Singapore, whose board of

00:17:06.250 --> 00:17:08.530
directors is formed by the PAP.

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:14.699
A CEO, after all, aims to maximise profit,
operate in global markets, but also keep work

00:17:14.699 --> 00:17:19.420
environments clean and orderly, and strive
to ensure the well-being of all his employees,

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:21.110
in this case, the citizens.

00:17:21.110 --> 00:17:22.280
It doesn’t end there.

00:17:22.280 --> 00:17:27.569
The media sector in Singapore is practically
a duopoly consisting of the state-owned Mediacorp

00:17:27.569 --> 00:17:32.980
and SPH Media, whose board members are members
or ex-politicians of the PAP.

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:37.100
Unsurprisingly, no news can be made public
without the express approval of the Ministry

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:38.120
of Information.

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:45.350
Moreover, since 2019, the government can censor
online content deemed "false" or "manipulated."

00:17:45.350 --> 00:17:49.530
Needless to say, if the opposition were to
contest censorship, they would be accused

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:50.640
of spreading fake news.

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:54.230
At this point, we might wonder: what do Singaporeans
think?

00:17:54.230 --> 00:17:59.580
According to a survey by the Pew Research
Center, an American think tank, 80% of Singaporeans

00:17:59.580 --> 00:18:03.309
are satisfied with how their democracy actually
functions.

00:18:03.309 --> 00:18:07.640
After all, the more positive the results achieved
by the "company" Singapore, the less inclined

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:09.170
its citizens will be to protest.

00:18:09.170 --> 00:18:10.950
Nothing wrong with that.

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:16.570
This paradigm fits well with Singaporean culture,
centered on the concept of kiasu, a term that

00:18:16.570 --> 00:18:18.740
roughly means "fear of losing."

00:18:18.740 --> 00:18:23.159
Losing not just a competition, but also an
opportunity, that is, the chance to work hard

00:18:23.159 --> 00:18:25.250
together to reach greater heights.

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:29.690
On the other hand, the population fears climate
change, which could submerge the city's coasts,

00:18:29.690 --> 00:18:33.920
and the recent rise in the cost of living
and real estate, considering also that Singapore

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:37.840
imports 90% of its food from abroad, mainly
from Malaysia.

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:42.520
Meanwhile, without noise or brutality, Singapore
continues undisturbed to limit dissent and

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:44.140
put a good face on a bad game.

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:48.660
Because, after all, Lee Kuan Yew and his successors
have literally legitimised a dystopia.

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:50.240
Let’s ask ourselves.

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:56.070
How are we doing, in our own society, our
own western society, if we come to admire

00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:57.289
a country like this?

00:18:57.289 --> 00:19:00.090
Now if you’ll excuse I have to go get my
dose of canings.

